  <HeroThesis kicker="This week's thesis">
    The instruments are losing fidelity. The dashboard the enterprise AI buyer used through 2025
    (public benchmarks, a converging lab-governance posture, hardware as the value-capture layer)
    diverged from the underlying system in the same week, on five independent surfaces.
    Procurement and policy now require gauges the prior twelve months did not need.
  </HeroThesis>

  <Synthesis>
    <p>
      The reading I have been carrying for two scans is that the unified industry surface of 2024
      to 2025 is fracturing. This week, the fracture became measurable on five independent
      surfaces in seven days. GPT-5.5 simultaneously took every headline benchmark and posted an
      eighty-five percent hallucination rate, the highest at the frontier. Google publicly
      inverted the Glasswing "withhold and coordinate" pattern with an unrestricted Department of
      War deal that includes safety-filter modifiability on government request. Moonshot AI's
      open-weight Kimi K2.6 declined to compete on the AAII leaderboard at all, leading instead on
      <em>agentic depth</em> (three hundred parallel subagents, four-thousand-step workflows). The
      Hugging Face EvalEval coalition reported that a single statistically reliable agent
      benchmark now costs between forty thousand and three hundred and twenty thousand dollars to
      run, transferring leaderboard authorship to whoever can afford the eval. And SemiAnalysis
      published evidence that hardware vendors are leaving margin on the table while model-lab
      inference margins move from thirty-eight to seventy percent.
    </p>

    <p>
      The convergence is the point. Each signal alone is consequential. Together, they describe a
      market where the four gauges the 2024-to-2025 procurement playbook depended on (public
      benchmark scores, $ / token comparisons, an assumed industry-wide governance posture, and
      hardware-as-value-capture) are <strong>all losing fidelity at the same time</strong>. The
      AAII-leader at the frontier is no longer the obvious enterprise default. The $ / token
      comparison, which the prior dispatch already called structurally wrong, now has a second
      axis of erosion in capability-versus-truthfulness. The unified
      withhold-and-coordinate posture that two prior scans corroborated (Anthropic Glasswing,
      OpenAI GPT-5.4-Cyber, DeepMind TCLs) just had a competing pole open. The hardware layer is
      not capturing the marginal value it produces.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the reader whose work is enterprise AI strategy: your procurement scorecard needs a
      second column beyond benchmark score and price. Truthfulness or hallucination rate,
      evaluation provenance, agentic depth on workloads you actually run, and
      government-modifiability terms in the vendor contract are now first-class diligence items,
      not edge cases. Your governance forecasting needs a multi-pole model rather than a
      converging-industry assumption. And the assumption that capacity rationing translates into
      hardware-vendor margin power, which the prior dispatch implied, needs to absorb the
      SemiAnalysis finding: the rents are moving, not to the silicon stack, but to the model-lab
      layer.
    </p>
  </Synthesis>

