    ]}
  >
    <p slot="why">
      Google reportedly signed an unrestricted "all lawful government purpose" agreement with the
      Department of War and committed to modifying safety filters at government request, with
      six-hundred-plus DeepMind employees signing an internal opposition letter, lending
      independent corroboration beyond the secondhand framing in Zvi's roundup. This is
      structurally significant in two ways. It inverts the Apr 14 Claude Mythos and Project
      Glasswing "withhold and coordinate" pattern that two other frontier labs (Anthropic, then
      OpenAI's GPT-5.4-Cyber) had appeared to be converging on, opening a genuine bifurcation in
      lab-government posture. And it makes "safety filter modifiability on demand" an explicit
      contractual term, which dissolves the assumption that frontier-model safety configurations
      are fixed at deployment. For enterprise AI procurement, this introduces a new diligence
      question, whether a vendor can be compelled to weaken safety configurations and under what
      conditions, that was implicit before this disclosure.
    </p>
    <p slot="watch">
      Whether Anthropic, OpenAI, or xAI publicly disclose comparable government-modifiability
      terms or explicitly disavow them within two quarters; convergence vs divergence here defines
      the industry posture. Whether NIST, EU AI Office, or UK AISI guidance starts requiring
      disclosure of "third-party safety-filter modification rights" in frontier-model procurement
      contracts. Either makes government-modifiability a first-class governance variable.
    </p>
