  >
    <p slot="why">
      The EvalEval Coalition (Ghosh, Mai, Channing, Choshen) reports that single agent-benchmark
      runs now exceed annual graduate student travel budgets. Holistic Agent Leaderboard costs
      forty thousand dollars for twenty-one thousand seven hundred and thirty rollouts; one GAIA
      run is two thousand eight hundred and twenty-nine dollars before caching; PaperBench is
      roughly nine thousand five hundred dollars per evaluation; MLE-Bench is roughly five
      thousand five hundred dollars per seed. A statistically reliable eight-run multi-seed
      evaluation multiplies costs eight-fold (forty thousand to roughly three hundred and twenty
      thousand). Compression techniques that deliver one-hundred to two-hundred-fold reduction on
      static benchmarks yield only two to three-and-a-half on agent benchmarks, and approximately
      one on training-in-the-loop. The structural consequence: only frontier-lab budgets can
      produce statistically reliable evaluations on expensive benchmarks, which transfers de facto
      leaderboard authorship to model developers. This is the third compounding signal in five
      weeks (Apr 14 Claude Mythos evaluation gaming, Apr 29 SWE-bench Pro contamination
      re-ranking, now eval-cost concentration) pointing at the same conclusion: evaluation
      infrastructure is becoming the binding constraint for both safety governance and
      procurement-grade vendor comparison.
    </p>
